<section id="yaml-vs" class="py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-50">
    YAML vs JSON vs TOML: Which Format Should You Use?
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Each data serialization format has its strengths. Here is how YAML compares to JSON and TOML for configuration and data exchange.</p>
  <div class="mt-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/50">
          <th class="text-left py-3.5 px-5 font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100">Feature</th>
          <th class="text-center py-3.5 px-5 font-semibold text-white bg-emerald-600 rounded-tl-lg">YAML</th>
          <th class="text-center py-3.5 px-5 text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">JSON</th>
          <th class="text-center py-3.5 px-5 text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">TOML</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-100 dark:divide-zinc-800">
        {[
          ['Human readability', 'Excellent', 'Good', 'Good'],
          ['Comments supported', 'Yes (# prefix)', 'No', 'Yes (# prefix)'],
          ['Anchors & aliases', 'Yes (& and *)', 'No', 'No'],
          ['Multi-document support', 'Yes (--- separator)', 'No (use array)', 'No'],
          ['Native date/time types', 'Yes', 'No', 'Yes'],
          ['Best for', 'Configuration, DevOps, CI/CD', 'API data exchange', 'Rust/Python project config'],
          ['Complexity', 'Medium (whitespace matters)', 'Low (brackets & commas)', 'Low'],
          ['Parsing speed', 'Slowest (complex spec)', 'Fastest', 'Fast'],
        ].map(([feature, ...vals]) => (
          <tr class="hover:bg-zinc-50/50 dark:hover:bg-zinc-900/30 transition-colors">
            <td class="py-3 px-5 font-medium text-zinc-800 dark:text-zinc-200">{feature as string}</td>
            {vals.map((v, i) => (
              <td class={`py-3 px-5 text-center text-sm ${i === 0 ? 'text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-300 font-medium' : 'text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400'}`}>{v as string}</td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
    <strong class="text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Bottom line:</strong> Use YAML when human readability and comment support matter (configs, CI/CD, K8s). Use JSON for machine-to-machine communication (REST APIs, webhooks). Use TOML for simple project configuration files, especially in the Rust and Python ecosystems. And when you need to switch between formats, our <a href="#formatter" class="text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 hover:underline">YAML to JSON converter</a> handles the translation instantly.
  </p>
</section>
